/** \page memory_arbiter The Memory Arbiter and its View classes

This page describes the concepts and the usage of the FEAT::Memory::Arbiter class as well as its two
closely related FEAT::Memory::View and FEAT::Memory::TypedView classes.

\tableofcontents

\section memarb_sec_motivation Motivation

When using a computer with some sort of dedicated accelerator hardware -- typically a GPU -- one has
to deal with the fact that the computer (or each individual compute node in case of a supercomputer
cluster) now has two disjoint memory pools, namely the "main" or "host" memory, which is only
accessible by the CPU, as well as the "device" memory, which is only accessible by the accelerator
hardware's processor, e.g. the GPU. In consequence, if, say, a matrix has been assembled in main
memory by the CPU and you then want to apply a fast linear solver that is running on the GPU on that
matrix, then the matrix has to be moved/copied from the host memory to the device memory via the
fairly slow PCI-express bus, because neither the CPU nor the GPU can directly access the other's
memory pools.

\image html images/memarb_cpu_gpu.svg

The problem is that this transfer between host and device memory does not happen automagically,
but instead it has to be performed explicitly by the programmer "by hand" whenever a memory transfer
is required in the algorithm. For various reasons it is desirable to minimize the amount of memory
transfers and the synchronizations between CPU and the accelerator that always have to be performed
as part of the memory transfer; in simple terms: you do not want to copy a memory region from host
to device memory if that same memory region has already been copied before and it's contents haven't
changed since the last memory transfer has been performed, but obviously you \e must perform a
memory transfer if the content \e has changed. Although this can be solved by very carefully
tracking the flow of memory throughout the algorithm design phase, which may be perfectly feasible
in fairly small specialist codes, but this approach is almost impossible to realize in a complex
mathematical software framework consisting of hundreds of nested templated classes for any
programmer who isn't on the same coding level as John Carmack.

There exist concepts which automate these memory transfers as part of the underlying accelerator
library (most notably Nvidia's managed memory support) on a page-by-page and access-by-access basic,
ultimately freeing the programmer from all the headache of having to think about different memory
pools and memory update patterns at all, but this functionality is not always available and, if it
is available, it comes with an overhead that ranges from moderate to astronomical depending on
various factors such as the accelerator type and the operating system (we have in fact measured a
slowdown of factor 400 in the most extreme case as compared to a manual memcopy).

Finally, the third possible solution to this problem is a middle way between the fully manual
memory transfer by the programmer and the fully automated memory management by the library discussed
above. This middle way still requires the programmer to explicitly request that data, e.g. the
arrays that make up a matrix, is expected to be present in host and/or device memory at a specific
step of the algorithm, but which leaves all the required book-keeping and the actual triggering of
memory transfers between host and device to either a full-grown memory manager class or to a more
light-weight smart-pointer class -- and the latter one is exactly what the memory arbiter in FEAT 3
implements.

\section memarb_sec_overview Overview

All the classes, enums and helper functions related to the memory arbiter implementation in FEAT 3
are located in the FEAT::Memory namespace. As mentioned in the motivation above, the memory arbiter
is effectively just a light-weight smart pointer class similar to the <c>std::shared_ptr</c> class
from the standard library, however, the memory arbiter can hold a copy of the data array in device
memory of some accelerator device, which is typically GPU whose resources can be used by either
Nvidia's CUDA library or AMD's HIP/ROCM library depending on whether you're using an Nvidia or AMD
GPU -- please note that at the time of writing, AMD HIP/ROCM support has not yet been added to
FEAT 3 and its memory arbiter classes, but the classes have been prepared so that this extension
is fairly easy to implement.

The memory arbiter has two important jobs to take care of: firstly, it needs to hold pointers to
multiple data arrays, which are identical copies but residing in different memory pools (host and
device[s]) and secondly, it needs to decide when a memory transfer between host and device needs to
be performed to ensure that the data array in the memory location that the user intends to use in
the next step of his/her algorithm contains an up-to-date state of the data array, no matter when
and where the last write operation to the data array was performed. The latter task is the one that
is tricky, because it requires some book-keeping on which memory location was last used to update
the memory array contents and to be able to accomplish this in an efficient manner without deep
interaction with the operating system's internal memory management, the user has to inform the
memory arbiter whenever he/she intends to modify the arbiter's internal data array, which could in
theory be done in a manual manner by the means of calling a member function of the arbiter that
informs it that its internal data array has been updated, but this approach tends to be very error
prone as users tend to forget to perform this call and these types of errors are almost impossible
to hunt down and therefore a different mechanism based on \e views was implemented in FEAT 3.

The memory arbiter holds the pointers to the arrays located in host and/or device memory, but it is
not possible for the user to ask for a host or device memory pointer directly, instead the user has
to request a \e view of the data for a specific memory location, i.e. host or device, with a
specific set of access rights, i.e. read-only, read-write or write-only access. The view is only
a small helper class that is usually instantiated as a <em>scope guard</em> object on the stack and
that notifies the memory arbiter that it belongs to when it is destroyed once it goes out of scope;
this allows for the automation of the necessary memory update/transfer book-keeping with minimal
interaction from the user and, more importantly, it minimizes the risk of memory leaks or forgotten
memory transfers by incorporating this functionality in the interaction of the memory arbiter and
its related memory view class. The view object then allows the user to query the raw pointer (either
in host or device memory) so that he/she can work directly on the raw data arrays without any
additional overhead introduced by getter/setter function calls or the like.

\section memarb_sec_arbiter The Memory Arbiter class

As already mentioned in the overview, the FEAT::Memory::Arbiter class can be interpreted as a
<em>smart pointer</em> class similar to <c>std::shared_ptr</c> that automatically synchronizes the
content of the memory regions that it manages between multiple memory locations, e.g. main aka.
host aka. system memory and CUDA aka. device aka. GPU memory. Technically, the FEAT::Memory::Arbiter
class only contains a pointer to an instance of an FEAT::Memory::Block object on the heap, which
contains and manages all the relevant pointers and book-keeping data, but the user never interacts
with the memory block object directly, but only via a memory arbiter or a memory view object.
The memory  block contains an internal reference counter, which is used to automatically free all
the allocated memory arrays of the block once the last memory arbiter referencing that memory block
is destroyed.

A memory arbiter object is usually created on the stack or as a class member variable and during
its constructor call, one at least hast to specify the number of bytes that are to be reserved for
the data that is to be stored and managed in the arbiter's memory block. Once the arbiter goes out
of scope, all of its allocated memory is freed unless another arbiter still holds a reference to
the arbiters memory block:

\code{.cpp}
{
  // create an arbiter for an array of 8 int objects
  Memory::Arbiter arb(8u*sizeof(int));

  // work with the arbiter's memory (see below)
  ...

  // once the arbiter goes out of scope, its memory is freed
}
\endcode

\subsection memarb_arbiter_alloc_init Allocation and Initialization

By default, the memory arbiter does not immediately allocate the memory as it procrastinates the
allocation until the first view is created by the user or until the user calls the
\ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::alloc "alloc" member function of the arbiter.
However, it is also possible to tell the arbiter that it should allocate the memory in host and/or
device memory upon construction.
Furthermore, one can also tell the constructor how the data array is to be initialized with the
following available options:

- FEAT::Memory::Init::uninitialized : Leave the memory block uninitialized and therefore its
  contents in an undefined state.

- FEAT::Memory::Init::format_to_zero : Format all bits of the memory block to zero.

- FEAT::Memory::Init::format_to_one : Format all bits of the memory block to one.

- FEAT::Memory::Init::dont_care : You don't give a damn about what the content of the memory block
  is initialized with.\n
  Note: this is equal to FEAT::Memory::Init::uninitialized in optimized builds
  and equal to FEAT::Memory::Init::format_to_one for debug builds.

\attention Please note that FEAT::Memory::Init::format_to_one formats all <b><u>bits</u></b> of the
memory block to one, which is fundamentally different from formatting all <b><u>array elements</u></b>
to one, e.g. if an array of \c int objects if formatted bit-wise to 1 then each array element will
have the value -1 according to [two's complement representation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two%27s_complement),
whereas an array of float or double objects that is formatted bit-wise to 1 has a value of
[NaN](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NaN) for each element according to the
[IEEE-754 encoding](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754) of floating point numbers.
This is in fact the intended behavior of \ref FEAT::Memory::Init::format_to_one "format_to_one",
as this allows to identify read accesses to uninitialized memory, which is otherwise hard to find
when the system's standard library formats uninitialized memory to zero.
If you want to format an array of floating point values to a given floating point value, e.g. 1.0,
then you have to use the \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::format() "format" member function of the
arbiter after its creation.

\code{.cpp}
// create an arbiter for an array of 8 int objects in main memory
// and format the all bits of the array to zero
Memory::Arbiter arb_1(8u*sizeof(int), Memory::Location::main, FEAT::Memory::Init::format_to_zero);

// create an arbiter for an array of 4 double objects in CUDA memory
// and format all bits of the array to one, which results in NaNs values
Memory::Arbiter arb_2(4u*sizeof(double), Memory::Location::cuda, FEAT::Memory::Init::format_to_one);

// work with the arbiter's memory
...
\endcode

\subsection memarb_arbiter_copies Shallow and Deep Copies of Memory Arbiters

Memory arbiters can be moved via the <c>std::move</c> function, but they cannot be copied using the
copy constructor as it is deleted, which is to prevent the compiler from creating unintended copies.
If one wants to create a <b>shallow copy</b> of an arbiter that shares the same memory block as the
source arbiter that it was copied from, then can use the \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::attach() "attach"
member function of the arbiter.
On the other hand, if one wants to create a <b>deep copy</b> that obtains an independent copy of the
memory block, then one can use the \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::clone() "clone" member function:

\code{.cpp}
// create an arbiter for 8 int's
Memory::Arbiter arb_1(8u*sizeof(int), Memory::Location::main);

... // format array of arb_1 to x[i] = i

// create a shallow copy of arb_1
Memory::Arbiter arb_2(arb_1.attach());

// create a deep copy of arb_1
Memory::Arbiter arb_3(arb_1.clone());

...
\endcode

In the code above, \c arb_1 and \c arb_2 are both attached to the same memory block, whereas \c arb_3
received an independent (deep) copy of the memory block referenced by \c arb_1 and \c arb_2.

\image html images/memarb_attach_clone.svg

It is also possible to attach or clone a sub-region of an arbiter's memory block rather than the
entire source memory block by specifying an offset and a size for the attach or clone function.
The following example creates a (source) memory arbiter consisting of 8 int objects and it attaches
another arbiter that only references the 4 elements starting at the 2nd position in the array
of the source arbiter:

\code{.cpp}
// create an arbiter for 8 int's
Memory::Arbiter arb_1(8u*sizeof(int), Memory::Location::main);

... // format array of arb_1 to x[i] = i

// create a shallow copy range of arb_1
Memory::Arbiter arb_2(arb_1.attach(2u*sizeof(int), 4u*sizeof(int));

...
\endcode

In the code above, \c arb_1 and \c arb_2 are both attached to the same memory block, however,
\c arb_2 only references the array elements [2, ..., 5]:

\image html images/memarb_attach_range.svg

In analogy, it is also possible to clone a range of a source memory arbiter, however, in this case
one has to decide whether the entire source memory block is to be cloned or only the sub-region that
is actually referenced by the memory arbiter; the default case is the latter one.

\code{.cpp}
// create an arbiter for 8 int's
Memory::Arbiter arb_1(8u*sizeof(int), Memory::Location::main);

... // format array of arb_1 to x[i] = i

// create a deep copy range of arb_1's elements 2 through 5
Memory::Arbiter arb_2(arb_1.clone(2u*sizeof(int), 4u*sizeof(int), false);

// only 4 int objects cloned into block
XASSERT(arb_2.block_bytes() ==  4u*sizeof(int)); // bytes in block
XASSERT(arb_2.bytes() ==  4u*sizeof(int)); // bytes referenced by arbiter

// create a deep copy of arb_1's array, but reference only elements 2 through 5
Memory::Arbiter arb_3(arb_1.clone(2u*sizeof(int), 4u*sizeof(int), true);

// all 8 int objects cloned into block, but only 4 referenced by arbiter
XASSERT(arb_3.block_bytes() == 8u*sizeof(int)); // bytes in block
XASSERT(arb_3.bytes() ==  4u*sizeof(int)); // bytes referenced by arbiter

...
\endcode
In the code above, \c arb_1, \c arb_2 and \c arb_3 all reference independent memory blocks, where
\c arb_2 references a memory block that contains only the four elements that were copied from the
range [2, ..., 5] of source array from \c arb_1, wheres \c arb_3 references a memory block that
contains copies of all 8 source array elements, but the arbiter \c arb_3 only references the
range [2, ..., 5] of that block:

\image html images/memarb_clone_range.svg

As already mentioned, it is possible to explicitly tell an arbiter, which has not been allocated
before, to allocate and optionally initialize its memory by manually calling the alloc member
function, which also requires a (combination of) memory location(s) and the initialization
parameters.

\subsection sec_memarb_arbiter_release Freeing Memory and Releasing Arbiters

It is typically not necessary to manually free an arbiter's resources as this is done automatically
when the arbiter is destroyed by the usual reference-counting smart-pointer magic, but in some cases
one wants to be able to either free the memory arrays for a specific memory location (e.g. to free
up some precious GPU memory) or to release the arbiter prematurely and to allow it to free all of
its attached resources unless another arbiter still references them, of course.
If you just want to free the data arrays allocated by an arbiter for a specific (combination of)
memory location(s), then you can use the \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::free "free" member function
to accomplish this. The arbiter will still be attached to the memory block, which now may not hold
any data arrays anymore, but the arbiter will still remain in a valid state and a subsequent call to
\ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::alloc "alloc" will re-allocate the arrays of the correct size that is
still stored in the arbiter, but obviously the previous contents of the memory block will be lost.

\attention
The \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::free "free" member function of the memory arbiter does not check
whether more than one memory arbiter is attached to the memory block whose data array is to be freed,
so it is in the user's responsibility to ensure that the data to be freed is not required elsewhere.

Furthermore, you can also release (or "detach") an arbiter from its memory block by calling the
\ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::release "release" member function, which will decrease the block's
reference counter and free up all memory if this was the last arbiter referencing this particular
block, and it will leave the arbiter in an empty state afterwards. Again, it is typically not
required to do this, however, in some cases -- especially when the arbiter object is a member
variable of some other object -- one sometimes wants to release the memory arbiter's data before
the object containing the memory arbiter has reached the end of its lifetime.

\section memarb_views Memory Views, Locations and Access Rights

The \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter "Memory::Arbiter" class offers various functions to format and copy
the memory regions referenced by the arbiter, but obviously one also needs some way to directly
access the raw data array in either host/main or device/cuda memory. As already mentioned, the
arbiter class does not offer any functions to \e directly access the data arrays of its memory
block, simply because the arbiter needs to perform some book-keeping to keep the data arrays in
host and device memory synchronized. Therefore, the only way to directly access the data arrays is
by first creating a \e view for the memory, which then directly offers access to the underlying
memory region via raw or typed pointers and element access operator overloads. The view acts
primarily as a scope guard that notifies the arbiter when a user is finished reading and/or writing
to its data array in a specific memory location. The secondary objective of the view is to act as
a guard against race conditions, especially in the context of multi-threading or overlapping
host-device algorithms to ensure that all memory locations are kept in synchronization whenever
a memory location is accessed by a view.

There are two view classes available for the memory arbiter system in FEAT, which are the
FEAT::Memory::View class and the FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T_> class template, where the latter one
is a derived class of the former one.
Internally, the memory arbiter's block stores <c>void*</c> pointers to its data arrays
and the (untemplated) \ref FEAT::Memory::View "View" class allows the user to query the size of the
data array in bytes by calling its \ref FEAT::Memory::View::bytes() "bytes" member function
and it allows to retrieve the raw \p void* pointer to the data array on the memory
location that the view was created for via the \ref FEAT::Memory::View::raw_r() "raw_r" member
function for a <c>const void*</c> pointer or via the \ref FEAT::Memory::View::raw_w() "raw_w"
member function for a mutable <c>void*</c> pointer.

The \ref FEAT::Memory::TypedView "TypedView" class template interprets the memory arbiter's data
array as an array of elements of type \c T_ and it offers functions to query the number of elements
of type \p T_ by calling the \ref FEAT::Memory::View::size() "size" member function, as well as
the member functions \ref FEAT::Memory::TypedView::get_r() "get_r" and
\ref FEAT::Memory::TypedView::get_w() "get_w" that return a <c>const T_*</c> and a mutable
<c>T_*</c> pointer to the data array, respectively. Furthermore, this class also offers the two
\ref FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator[]() "operator[]" and
\ref FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator()() "operator()" overloads, which both offer array element
access; the \ref FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator[]() "operator[]" offers both a const and a
mutable overload, whereas \ref FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator()() "operator()" offers only
a const version and is therefore recommended for read-only element access.

\attention
It is important to note that FEAT::Memory::View::raw_w(), FEAT::Memory::TypedView::get_w() as well
as the mutable overload of FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator[]() will throw a
FEAT::Memory::AccessException if the view was not created with FEAT::Memory::Access::write or
FEAT::Memory::Access::read_write access rights, see \ref sec_memarb_access. If you created a view
with read-only access rights, then be sure to use the FEAT::Memory::View::raw_r() and
FEAT::Memory::TypedView::get_r() member functions as well as the FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator()()
for pointer retrieval and element access, respectively. Also remember that the const overload of
FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator[]() will only be called by the compiler if you actually declared
the view object itself as const -- using FEAT::Memory::TypedView::operator()() for read-only access
circumvents this problem; see also the first example in \ref sec_memarb_access.

Memory Views are designed to be fairly short-lived objects, which are typically created on the stack
whenever an algorithm requires direct access to a memory arbiter's data, and which are destroyed
by either going out of scope or by being explicitly released by calling its
\ref FEAT::Memory::View::release "release" member function immediately after the algorithm is
finished reading/writing the arbiter's data array contents. In many cases, an algorithm reads/writes
to the same data array at different stages and in this case it is usually advisable to create a new
view for each of these stages. It is possible to create views as member variables of some class,
but in this case one has to keep in mind that the mapped views will prevent any write-views to be
created for the same arbiter as long as their encapsulating object is not destroyed or until the
views are released manually, therefore storing views as member variables should be avoided unless
the encapsulating object is meant to be a short-lived object itself.

\subsection sec_memarb_view_move Moving Views

Instances of the FEAT::Memory::View class are moveable, but they are not copyable and they cannot be
attached, shallow copied or deep cloned or anything similar like that. If a view object is moved into
another view object that is still mapped, then the destination view will be first released before
the source view is moved into it, i.e. there is nothing special to keep in mind when moving views.

The move semantics of the FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T_> class template are a bit more interesting:
- It is possible to move-construct or move-assign a FEAT::Memory::View object from a
  FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T_> object regardless of the chosen type \c T_. (This simply a direct
  consequence from the fact that the TypedView class template is derived from the View class.)
- It is possible to move-construct or move-assign a FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T_> object from a
  FEAT::Memory::View object regardless of the chosen type \c T_.
- It is possible to move-construct or move-assign a FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T_> object from another
  FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T_> object if and only if the source and destination are instanced
  for the same type \c T_.
- It is \b not possible to move-construct or move-assign a FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T1_> object from
  another FEAT::Memory::TypedView<T2_> object for any type T2_ != T1_.

The forbidden <em>typed view conversion</em> mentioned in the last point above is explicitly deleted
by intention, as the conversion of typed views of different type is usually an unwanted error case
and therefore allowing it would lead to hard-to-find bugs in many cases.
If you \e really need to convert one <c>TypedView<T1_></c> object into another <c>TypedView<T2_></c>
object for T1_ != T2_, e.g. when in the case <c>T1_ = signed int</c> and <c>T2_ = unsigned int</c>,
then you can accomplish this by jumping through one extra hoop and wrapping the source object in
an <c>std::forward<Memory::View&&></c> call, i.e.:

\code{.cpp}
Memory::Arbiter arb(64u);

// create a signed int view
Memory::TypedView<signed int> view_si(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read_write));

// move-convert to unsigned int view
Memory::TypedView<unsigned int> view_ui(std::forward<Memory::View&&>(std::move(view_si)));
\endcode

In analogy to the View class, it should be obvious that TypedView objects are also uncopyable and
they cannot be attached, shallow copied or deep cloned or anything similar like that.

\subsection sec_memarb_location Memory Locations

Whenever you desire to directly access to the data array of a memory arbiter's block, you obviously
need to decide whether you require a host (main) memory pointer, that you can use to modify directly
from your C++ code, or whether you require a device (cuda) pointer that you can pass a CUDA library
function or to a self-written CUDA kernel. When creating a view by calling the arbiters
\ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::view() "view" or \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::typed_view() "typed_view"
member function, you simply have to specify the desired FEAT::Memory::Location enum value as the
first function argument to obtain a view for that specific location and that view will then grant
you access to the raw host (main) or device (cuda) pointer depending on which memory location the
view was created for.

Currently, the only two options that you have is FEAT::Memory::Location::main, which represent the
"normal" host memory accessible to your C++ code, and FEAT::Memory::Location::cuda, which represents
the "device" memory for a CUDA device for access by CUDA kernels or CUDA library functions.
In future, this may be extended by support for other accelerator memory pools such as ROCM/HIP or
something else.

\subsection sec_memarb_access Memory Access Rights

The second question that you have to ask yourself when creating a view for a memory arbiter's data
array for a specific memory location is which access rights you require for your algorithm:

- Do you require <b>read-and-write</b> access, because your algorithm may read and may overwrite
  (some parts of) the data array?
- Do you require <b>read-only</b> access, because your algorithm only reads the contents of the data
  array but it never modifies it?
- Do you require <b>write-only</b> access, because your algorithm <b>overwrites <u>the
  entire data array</u></b> without reading its previous content? (see attention note further below)

The answer to this question serves two purposes: firstly, it is used to prevent race conditions
in multi-threaded or host-device overlapping codes and, secondly and more importantly, it is
required by the arbiter's internal logic to determine when a memory transfer between host and
device memory has to be performed, because for this the arbiter needs to know when the memory
content might have been modified in one location and that therefore a memory transfer has to be
initiated to upload the modified contents to another memory location when a read view for that
location is to be created -- this is part of the book-keeping magic that we have mentioned a
few times already.
The desired memory access is specified as a (combination of) FEAT::Memory::Access enum value(s)
as the second argument when calling the arbiter's \ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::view() "view" or
\ref FEAT::Memory::Arbiter::typed_view() "typed_view" member function.

There are three basic rules that determine how access rights are granted:

- <u><b>Rule #1:</b></u> A write-only or read-write access view for a memory arbiter can only
be created if there are currently no other views mapped for that particular memory arbiter.

- <u><b>Rule #2:</b></u> If a write-only or read-write access view is mapped for a memory
arbiter, then no other view can be mapped to that particular arbiter until the write access view
is released.

- <u><b>Rule #3:</b></u> Thirdly, as long as no write-only or read-write access view are
mapped for a memory arbiter, one may create as many read-only views for that particular memory
arbiter simultaneously on all available memory locations as one desires and the arbiter ensures
that all requested memory locations are up-to-date.

\attention
When requesting a view with only FEAT::Memory::Access::write access rights (i.e. <u>not</u>
FEAT::Memory::Access::read_write), then the arbiter <b>silently assumes that you will overwrite
the <u>entire</u> data array accessible by the view</b> and therefore the arbiter \e may decide to
not update the data array if the requested memory location is out-of-date before granting the view.
If you intent to only overwrite parts of the memory region accessible by the view, e.g. as part of
a \e scatter operation, then you <u><b>must</b></u> request FEAT::Memory::Access::read_write access
even if you do not intend to read any data from the array, just to ensure that the arbiter
synchronizes the memory locations (if necessary) before you starting writing values to the data
array.\n
<b>If in doubt</b>, then request a view with FEAT::Memory::Access::read_write access rights; in the
worst case this might throw an exception in your face if two views for the same memory block are
created concurrently, but it will at least ensure that the data arrays you are given access to are
synchronized over all memory locations at the cost of (potentially) unnecessary memory transfers.

\note
When requesting a view with only FEAT::Memory::Access::write access rights for an arbiter that
references a sub-region of the memory block's entire data array, i.e. when the arbiter's offset
is > 0 and/or its size less than the entire block size minus the arbiter offset (see also
\ref memarb_arbiter_copies) then the arbiter will automatically extend the requested access to
\ref FEAT::Memory::Access::read_write "read_write" to ensure that the memory outside of the
arbiter's sub-range is synchronized, as it cannot be entirely overwritten by the write-only view.
In simple terms: you do not need to worry whether an arbiter is a sub-arbiter of another
arbiter when asking for write-only access to overwrite the sub-arbiters entire data range.

The following example creates two memory arbiters for arrays of 8 \c int objects, then initializes
the first array as x[i] = i via a simple for loop, then performs a memcopy in cuda device memory
from the first to the second arbiter and finally writes the contents of the second arbiter to the
console:

\code{.cpp}
const std::size_t n = 8;

// create two arbiters for an array of 8 int's
Memory::Arbiter arb_1(n * sizeof(int));
Memory::Arbiter arb_2(n * sizeof(int));

// create a typed view with write access in main memory and initialize the array elements
{
  Memory::TypedView<int> view(arb_1.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));
  for(std::size_t i(0); i < n; ++i)
    view[i] = i; // use operator[] which is mutable
}

// create two raw views in cuda memory to copy arb_1's array to arb_2's array
{
  const Memory::View view_1(arb_1.view(Memory::Location::cuda, Memory::Access::read)); // [1]
  Memory::View view_2(arb_2.view(Memory::Location::cuda, Memory::Access::write));
  Memory::memcopy_cuda(arb_2.raw_w(); arb_1.raw_r(), arb_1.bytes());
}

// create a typed view with read access in main memory for arb_2 and print to console
{
  const Memory::TypedView<int> view(arb_2.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read)); // [2]
  for(std::size_t i(0); i < n; ++i)
    std::cout << view(i) << "\n"; // use operator() which is const
}
\endcode

In the code above, only two memory transfers are performed: in the line marked with <c>[1]</c>,
the host memory array of \c arb_1 is copied into its device memory array, and in the line
marked with <c>[2]</c>, the device memory array of \c arb_2 is copied into its host memory array.

As already mentioned above, memory views are meant to be as short-lived as possible and so it is
strongly recommended to put a block scope as small as possible directly around each algorithm
segment that reads or writes data from/to a memory arbiter's block. Alternatively, if this results
in a mess of nested block scopes, it is also possible to release a view prematurely by calling its
\ref FEAT::Memory::View::release "release" member function. Note that you cannot directly
<em>re-open</em> a view that has been released manually, but you can move a new view into the same
view object if required and therefore reduce the number of temporarily opened view objects in a
more complex algorithm:

\code{.cpp}
// create an arbiter for 64 bytes
Memory::Arbiter arb(64u);

// create a write view
Memory::View v(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));

// do something with the view
...

// release view manually
v.release();

// do something else
...

// "re-open" closed view by assigning a new view to it
v = arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write);

// do something more with the re-opened view
...
\endcode

\section sec_memarb_overlap Overlapping Memory Views

There exist some scenarios, in which it is either advantageous or even straight-out necessary to
create multiple views with potentially different access rights on potentially different memory
locations for one and the same memory block, but obviously only a subset of the whole cross product
of access rights and memory locations can result in a well-defined state after all the views are
destroyed.

One example where overlapping views are necessary is multi-threaded assembly, where multiple threads
are allowed to write to the same memory region at the same time, but then the assembly is responsible
for preventing data race conditions by some means of thread synchronization or data partitioning.

Another example, which is more of a convenience issue, is that for some algorithms, the output
memory range might be identical to one of the input memory ranges and without overlapping views one
would have to handle a plethora of different access rights by a potentially lengthy if-else cascade;
a concrete example is the AXPY operation r := y + alpha*x, where four different cases could occur:
- r, x and y are three different objects and therefore refer to different memory arbiters
- r and x are the same object, but y is a different object
- r and y are the same object, but x is a different object
- r and x and y are the same object
- Note that the case r != x = y is fairly uninteresting, as x and y are only read but not written to
  and read views are implicitly allowed to overlap.

So, to obey the memory access rules stated in \ref sec_memarb_access one would have to write the
following:
\code{.cpp}
void axpy(Memory::Arbiter& r, Memory::Arbiter& y, Memory::Arbiter& x, DataType alpha)
{
  if((r == x) && (r == y))
  {
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> r_view(r.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read_write));
    axpy_impl(r_view, r_view, r_view, alpha);
  }
  else if(r == x)
  {
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> r_view(r.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read_write));
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> y_view(y.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
    axpy_impl(r_view, y_view, r_view, alpha);
  }
  else if(r == y)
  {
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> r_view(r.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read_write));
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> x_view(x.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
    axpy_impl(r_view, r_view, x_view, alpha);
  }
  else // x and y may or may not be the same arbiter
  {
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> r_view(r.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> x_view(x.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
    Memory::TypedView<DataType> y_view(y.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
    axpy_impl(r_view, y_view, x_view, alpha);
  }
}
\endcode

In consequence, it is necessary to provide the competent programmer with a method to
bend the three rules stated it \ref sec_memarb_access a little bit. As stated in the third rule,
it is possible to create overlapping read views, but one can also create overlapping write-only
and/or read-write views by explicitly OR-combining the memory access flags with
FEAT::Memory::Access::overlap, however, even then there are some rules in place:

<u><b>Overlapping Write Rule #1:</b></u> If there are no views currently mapped to a memory arbiter,
then an overlapping write (write-only or read-write) access view can be created for that arbiter.

<u><b>Overlapping Write Rule #2:</b></u> If there is at least one overlapping write-access view
currently mapped to a memory arbiter for a specific memory location, then another overlapping
write-only or read-write or read-only view for the same memory location can be created for that
particular memory arbiter.

The two rules above imply the following corollaries:

- Overlapping write/read views can only be created for the same memory location.
- If overlapping write/read views are to be created for a memory arbiter, then the first (of the
  overlapping) views that is created must be an overlapping write-only or read-write view;
  in other words: If there is a non-overlapping write view or a read-only view mapped to an arbiter
  then no overlapping write views can be created for that particular arbiter until those views are
  released first.
- If you want to overlap a read and a write access view, then the overlapping write access view
  must be created first and the read access view second; the other way is not allowed.
- Read views are implicitly overlapping, i.e. it is not necessary to include the
  FEAT::Memory::Access::overlap when creating an overlapping read view.

With overlapping write views, the AXPY example from above can be handled in a single case,
where we have to create an overlapping write view for r as the very first step before creating
(potentially overlapping) read views for x and y:
\code{.cpp}
void axpy(Memory::Arbiter& r, Memory::Arbiter& y, Memory::Arbiter& x, DataType alpha)
{
  Memory::TypedView<DataType> r_view(r.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
  Memory::TypedView<DataType> x_view(x.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
  Memory::TypedView<DataType> y_view(y.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
  axpy_impl(r_view, y_view, x_view, alpha);
}
\endcode

Here are some examples of what overlapping combinations are allowed and which result in an error:
\code{.cpp}

// create an arbiter for 64 bytes:
Memory::Arbiter arb(64u);

// OK: read-only views are implicitly allowed to overlap as wildly as desired
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
  Memory::View view_3(arb.view(Memory::Location::cuda, Memory::Access::read));
  Memory::View view_4(arb.view(Memory::Location::cuda, Memory::Access::read));
}

// OK: three explicitly overlapping write views in same memory location
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
  Memory::View view_3(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read_write | Memory::Access::overlap));
}

// OK: explicitly overlapping write view allows read views to overlap on same memory location
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
}

// ERROR: write views are not implicitly allowed to overlap
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));
}

// ERROR: write and read views are not implicitly allowed to overlap
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
}

// ERROR: (overlapping) write cannot be created if a read view already exists
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::read));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
}

// ERROR: two overlapping write views in different memory locations are not allowed
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::cuda, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
}

// ERROR: read view cannot be mapped to a different memory location
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::cuda, Memory::Access::read));
}

// ERROR: first write view is not overlapping
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
}

// ERROR: second write view is not overlapping
{
  Memory::View view_1(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write | Memory::Access::overlap));
  Memory::View view_2(arb.view(Memory::Location::main, Memory::Access::write));
}
\endcode

As a final remark let it be said that the ability to overlap write views should be seen more as a
\e workaround where the programmer takes responsibility to ensure that whatever he/she decides to
do with the memory does not result in an inconsistent state between memory locations and that it
does not result in a race condition, which can be pretty tricky depending on the algorithm. The
usual non-overlapping memory access rules as stated in \ref sec_memarb_access exist for a reason and
they are meant to help you by preventing many commonly overseen race conditions and therefore it
is strongly advised that one only creates overlapping write-access views if that is either strictly
necessary or circumventing them would result in lengthy if-else cascades and if the effects of
overlapping write views are perfectly clear to you. You have been warned.

\author Peter Zajac

*/
